        "Health and wellness research has evolved significantly from a biomedical focus on disease treatment to a biopsychosocial model emphasizing prevention, health promotion, and holistic well-being. Salutogenic theory, proposed by Antonovsky (1979), marked a pivotal shift by exploring factors that support human health rather than those that cause disease, introducing the concept of a sense of coherence as central to effective coping and resilience. This paradigm has informed contemporary public health interventions, workplace wellness programs, and clinical practice guidelines that integrate physical, psychological, and social dimensions of health. Epidemiological studies have established robust associations between lifestyle factors, including regular physical activity, dietary patterns, sleep quality, stress management, and social connectedness, and long-term health outcomes such as cardiovascular disease incidence, metabolic syndrome prevalence, and all-cause mortality. The social determinants of health framework further highlights how factors such as socioeconomic status, education, housing, and access to healthcare shape health disparities across populations. Recent research has also illuminated the bidirectional relationship between gut microbiome composition and mental health, opening new avenues for understanding the mind-body connection. This growing body of evidence underscores the importance of interdisciplinary approaches to health promotion that address both individual behaviors and the structural, environmental, and social determinants of well-being in pursuit of health equity.",
